esp32s31: DRM_ESP32S31_GEM_CREATE - let a caller ask for a CACHED GEM buffer

The port tree is gitignored, so these are whole modified files rather than
a diff. Copy them back over linux-71-port/ before building.

  drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-lcd.c
  include/uapi/drm/esp32s31_drm.h

MODE_CREATE_DUMB has no way to express the CPU mapping and always gives
write-combine. That is right for a scanout buffer the CPU only writes and
wrong for a surface it also READS - an indexed game surface, or the
expanded result a compositor reads every frame. This ioctl sets
map_noncoherent via a .gem_create_object hook (the helper is handed only
device and size, so the choice reaches it through a per-device flag held
across the one call under gem_alloc_lock).

The pitch is tight, matching MODE_CREATE_DUMB: every consumer indexes
these buffers as w*h with no stride, so padding rows would put the
hardware and the CPU on different pixels at any width not a multiple of
four.

STATUS: BUILT AND SHIPPED, BUT NOT SHOWN TO PAY. It was written to remove
a write-combine penalty that turned out not to exist - at 320x200 a WC
index plane measures within 0.05% of a cached one end to end
(docs/sdl-acceleration-plan.md). It is kept because it is the only way to
express the choice at all. lvdesk leaves it off by default; XSHIM_GEM_CACHED
selects it (1 = index plane, 2 = expanded plane, 3 = both).

WHAT WAS TRIED AND REVERTED, SAME DAY: gating the PPA CLUT ioctl's
dma_sync_* calls on map_noncoherent, on the theory that a write-combine
buffer has no cache lines and syncing it is a pointless 192 KB cache walk
per frame. IT IS NOT POINTLESS. dma_alloc_wc on this SoC hands back CACHED
memory - only the USERSPACE mapping is write-combine - so the syncs are
load-bearing, and skipping them put horizontal bands of stale pixels
through the Doom window, visible on the physical panel. The driver already
said so in the scanout allocation's comment; the recorded knowledge was
correct and was ignored. The comment in the ioctl now says not to try it
again.

That cache maintenance is real work the hardware path owes and the CPU
path does not, and it is part of why the PPA only breaks even at 320x200.
